Here the copies are made while walking the normal `next` chain. Once a node has a copy in the map, its two links can be assigned immediately. A missing random pointer simply becomes `undefined`, which is the default value for the new node's `random` field.

The function does not alter the original list. This is handy when the input may be checked after the call.

```javascript
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    constructor(val = 0, next = null, random = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
        this.random = random;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    copyRandomList(head) {
        if (head === null) {
            return null;
        }

        const copies = new Map();
        let current = head;

        while (current !== null) {
            copies.set(current, new ListNode(current.val));
            current = current.next;
        }

        current = head;
        while (current !== null) {
            const copy = copies.get(current);
            copy.next = current.next === null ? null : copies.get(current.next);
            copy.random = current.random === null ? null : copies.get(current.random);
            current = current.next;
        }

        return copies.get(head);
    }
}
```

Both passes visit the list once, so the running time is `O(n)`. The map needs `O(n)` extra space.

A hash map keeps the matching node for every original node. The first pass creates all copied nodes. The second pass fills both `next` and `random` by looking up the target nodes in the map.

For example, if the list is `7 -> 13` and node `7` points randomly to `13`, the map gives the copy of `13` when that random link is assigned. The empty list is handled before the loops.

```java
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;
    ListNode random;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
        random = null;
    }

    ListNode(int val) {
        this.val = val;
        next = null;
        random = null;
    }

    ListNode(int val, ListNode next, ListNode random) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
        this.random = random;
    }
}
 */

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public ListNode copyRandomList(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        Map<ListNode, ListNode> copies = new HashMap<>();
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            copies.put(current, new ListNode(current.val));
            current = current.next;
        }

        current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            ListNode copy = copies.get(current);
            copy.next = copies.get(current.next);
            copy.random = copies.get(current.random);
            current = current.next;
        }

        return copies.get(head);
    }
}
```

Time complexity is `O(n)`. The map uses `O(n)` extra space.

The copy nodes are placed between the original nodes first. For an original node `current`, its copy is `current->next`, so the random pointer can be connected with `current->random->next`.

After all links are ready, the two lists are separated. This keeps extra space constant, but it temporarily changes the input list while the function runs.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode *random;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
        random = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
        random = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1, ListNode* r)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
        random = r;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* copyRandomList(ListNode* head) {
        if (head == NULL) {
            return NULL;
        }

        ListNode* current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            ListNode* copy = new ListNode(current->val);
            copy->next = current->next;
            current->next = copy;
            current = copy->next;
        }

        current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            if (current->random != NULL) {
                current->next->random = current->random->next;
            }
            current = current->next->next;
        }

        ListNode* copyHead = head->next;
        current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            ListNode* copy = current->next;
            current->next = copy->next;

            if (copy->next != NULL) {
                copy->next = copy->next->next;
            }
            current = current->next;
        }

        return copyHead;
    }
};
```

Time: `O(n)` where `n` is the number of nodes. Space: `O(1)` apart from the new copied nodes.

The original nodes are given indexes in a map, and the copied nodes are stored in a slice at those indexes. A second walk connects the links using the indexes. This avoids trying to copy a pointer directly, especially when `random` points backward or to the same node.

For a one-node list with `random` pointing to itself, the index lookup returns `0`, so the copied node points to itself as well. A nil input returns nil.

```go
/*
Definition for singly-linked list.
type ListNode struct {
    Val    int
    Next   *ListNode
    Random *ListNode
}
*/

func CopyRandomList(head *ListNode) *ListNode {
    if head == nil {
        return nil
    }

    indexes := make(map[*ListNode]int)
    originals := make([]*ListNode, 0)
    current := head

    for current != nil {
        indexes[current] = len(originals)
        originals = append(originals, current)
        current = current.Next
    }

    copies := make([]*ListNode, len(originals))
    for i, original := range originals {
        copies[i] = &ListNode{Val: original.Val}
    }

    for i, original := range originals {
        if original.Next != nil {
            copies[i].Next = copies[indexes[original.Next]]
        }
        if original.Random != nil {
            copies[i].Random = copies[indexes[original.Random]]
        }
    }

    return copies[0]
}
```

The two walks and the node creation take `O(n)` time. The index map, slices, and copied nodes take `O(n)` space.

